 terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 5b_{1} - 2b_{2} \\\\\n&=-2b_{2} + 5b_{1} \\\\\n&= 2(-b_{2}) - \\frac{5}{3}(-3b_{1}) \\\\\n&= 2c_{1} - \\frac{5}{3}c_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535455 false] [17592186119562 1100 "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/1e4b0e4345ef6d0bd0b350cc973eaa7c15b654e76b06cd6f42740af0ab8fc877.png) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2, \\mathbf{c}_3 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = -3\\mathbf{b}_2,$ $\\mathbf{c}_2=-\\mathbf{b}_3,$ and $\\mathbf{c}_3 = -4\\mathbf{b}_1?$" 13194139535455 true] [17592186119562 1100 "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2, \\mathbf{c}_3 \\right\\}$, where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = -3\\mathbf{b}_2$, $\\mathbf{c}_2=-\\mathbf{b}_3$, and $\\mathbf{c}_3 = -4\\mathbf{b}_1$?" 13194139535455 false] [17592186119562 1145 "Since ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/a5e125038beb90ff4fd2b16bea5b08f83855c80f2a70a73ccfc15fa53fd45a30.png) we can write $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1, \\mathbf c_2,$ and $\\mathbf c_3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3} \\\\  & =-3{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3}-2{b}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3{b}_{2})-5(-{b}_{3})+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{b}_{1}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}-5{c}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/refe